Say how the site actually deploys, because the README now says the opposite

#59 was written while Cloudflare's git connection was broken, and it argued for
moving the deploy into Actions. You fixed the connection instead — deleted the
project, remade it, reconnected — and the README on `main` now tells the next
reader to do something you deliberately did not do. My error, merged.

## What is now true, and what this says

**Cloudflare's git connection builds and publishes the site.** The section
carries the actual settings, which came up twice today because they were not
written down anywhere findable:

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Build command | `bundle exec jekyll build` |
| Deploy command | `npx wrangler deploy` |
| Non-production branch deploy command | `npx wrangler versions upload` |
| Path / root directory | `/` |
| Build output directory | `_site` |

With the reason `/` matters: that one field is both the working directory and
where wrangler starts looking for config, and pointing it at `worker/` would
deploy the **broker** over the site.

**The Actions path is described as what it now is — a spare, kept unarmed.** It
publishes nothing while `CLOUDFLARE_PAGES_TOKEN` is unset, which it is. The
section says plainly that the two are an **either/or**: set that secret while
the git connection is live and both will publish on every push.

## Three things that were wrong or missing

**The token needs no zone scope.** I told you to scope it to
`fcpublicmedia.org`. Wix holds DNS, so there is no Cloudflare zone to scope to.
Account-scoped only.

**A CNAME is enough for a custom hostname, and I said it was not.** I claimed a
Wix CNAME pointing at `workers.dev` could not get a certificate. It can, and
yours has:

```
new.fcpublicmedia.org → HTTP 200 · server: cloudflare
subject=CN=new.fcpublicmedia.org · issuer=Google Trust Services WE1 · expires Nov 15 2026
```

That is a real cert for a hostname Cloudflare is not authoritative for. So
there is now a section saying it out loud: **"get Cloudflare serving" and "put
the new site on the real domain" are separate**, no nameserver change is
needed, and only the second is a cutover. The opposite is easy to assume and
would turn a DNS record into a migration.

**Preview URLs are not lost.** #59 said moving to Actions would cost them. The
non-production deploy command is `npx wrangler versions upload`, so the git
connection gives you per-branch previews — which is now written down as a
reason to prefer it.

## The submodule theory is downgraded, not deleted

It read as settled and it is not. Recreating the project is what actually
cleared the fault, before anybody proved why, so the text now says the
asymmetry is *suggestive rather than conclusive* and adds the more useful
advice: **recreating the project is the cheap first move if it happens again.**

The one hard warning survives — do not add `submodules: true` to the checkout
in `deploy.yml`. Nothing in `_site` comes from `.advocate-engine`.

## Not done

The dormant Actions deploy is left in place. Removing it is a real option and
it is yours: it is a second path that can only fire if you arm it, which is
also true of the Azure block sitting above it, and the vendors advocate has
already filed the general shape of that concern. Say the word and it goes.
